A||||8||Zoom88||Painting Sail Panels...any experiences?||||1||
Z||000000||Zoom88||12-04-2001||08:07 AM|||| I am wondering if anyone has painted the<BR>sail panels on their car. Not the panels themselves but the part that houses the panels. I can not find any that are not weathered on the edges. I have recently bought 2 different sets which are in good condition but not any better than whats on the car now. I am not sure if painting would be a good solution, don't know if it would hold up or what type of paint to use. Any suggestions, I just can't keep spending money on sail panels !!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||12-04-2001||08:28 AM||||You should have painted your first ones. The best way is with the housing removed from car and without the panel itself. Sand it to remove old dust and for better adhesion. I think  I used Plastikote products. One is an adhesion improver (clear thing) and then 2-3 coats of semi gloss black. These are bumper paint products available at my PepBoys. Results were excellent. Looks like new. Have pics if interested. Don't forget to put a new Pontiac crest available from Rodney or FS.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000002||tesmith66||12-04-2001||08:37 AM||||I would like to see those pictures. I have the replacement panels from Paul McKibben and the crests from Rodney. All I need now is the time and the paint.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||10
Z||000003||Formula88||12-04-2001||09:47 AM||||Yes, please post your pics!  I'd like to see how they turned out.  <P>According to Paul McKibben and Ed Parks (with info from Paul's website, too), you want to use a Satin Black.  He recommends:<BR>Mar-Hyde Satin Black (Fiero on the can)<BR>PlastiCote Satin Black<BR>DupliColor T8 Satin Black.<P>I'm about to repaint mine with the PlastiCote Satin Black, but I'd like to see how the semi-gloss looks, too.  Any clear coat or top coat on it?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Zoom88||12-04-2001||12:36 PM|||| I have tried whatever brand it is that Walmart sells suppose to be for plastics, on my rocker panels they got scuff marks on them the first day I put them back on. I am not real pleased with them especially after all the time I spent. Needs some kinda of sealer or hardner top coat.||||reg||1
Z||000005||pherder||12-04-2001||01:11 PM||||Here is a side view of my old 88 Coupe (now Standard's 88 Coupe.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>The frame of the quarter "window" has been painted the same as the rest of the car.  Only the plexiglass (from Paul McKibben) is dark.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88Side.jpg"> <P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierobruiser||12-05-2001||01:24 AM||||I cleaned and taped off my good sail panel with masking tape and also taped the one that I had removed the broken plexiglas from and then repainted with Eastwood's black trim paint. It's a semi-gloss that looks great. I also used it on my engine vent covers. They all look like new. If you haven't seen their catalogue, there have a ton of great car restoration products available. <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com/cgi-bin/sgdynamo.exe?HTNAME=default2.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eastwoodco.com/cgi-bin/sgdynamo.exe?HTNAME=default2.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
